1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,600
A passing check can still be wrong.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,520
Here is a change to a program,
and here are its tests.

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:08,440
Every test passes.

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:10,520
The change is still wrong.

5
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:13,120
None of the tests ever
runs the line that broke.

6
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,960
A pass tells you the check found nothing.

7
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:18,880
It does not tell you the check
could have found it.

8
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,988
A common stand-in for a good
test suite is coverage:

9
00:00:22,988 --> 00:00:24,720
how much of the code the tests run.

10
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:29,532
Laura Inozemtseva and Reid Holmes
built thirty-one thousand test suites

11
00:00:29,532 --> 00:00:34,220
for five large Java programs and measured
how many planted faults each one caught.

12
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:36,900
Once they allowed for the size of a suite,

13
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:40,020
coverage was only weakly
to moderately related to catching faults.

14
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:43,400
In one program the link
was essentially zero.

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,020
Their advice: coverage
should not be used as a quality target.

16
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:50,407
One way to test a test
is to break the code

17
00:00:50,407 --> 00:00:52,860
on purpose and see
whether the tests notice.

18
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,180
The deliberate breaks are called mutants.

19
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:58,955
Rene Just and colleagues took
three hundred and fifty-seven real bugs

20
00:00:58,955 --> 00:01:02,400
that developers had fixed, and asked
whether mutants behave like them.

21
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,200
Seventy-three percent of the real bugs
were coupled to mutants.

22
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:09,014
Seventeen percent were coupled to none,

23
00:01:09,014 --> 00:01:11,640
so a check on the check
has blind spots of its own.

24
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:15,321
Coding benchmarks for AI
count a task as solved

25
00:01:15,321 --> 00:01:17,420
when the patch passes the task's tests.

26
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:22,580
A 2025 study re-examined patches
from three tools on five hundred tasks.

27
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:25,894
Seven point eight percent
of the patches counted as solved

28
00:01:25,894 --> 00:01:27,911
failed the developers' own tests,

29
00:01:27,911 --> 00:01:31,700
and twenty-nine point six percent
behaved differently from the real fix.

30
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:33,770
An earlier study hand-checked

31
00:01:33,770 --> 00:01:36,540
two hundred and fifty-one passing patches
from one agent.

32
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,240
In about a third,
the fix was already written

33
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:42,780
in the task description,
and about another third passed weak tests.

34
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:44,840
Both studies are preprints.

35
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:48,680
They show how far a pass can sit
from a fix, on these benchmarks.

36
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:53,780
So before trusting a check,
feed it something you know is wrong.

37
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:56,700
If a broken input passes,
the check is broken.

38
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:01,660
That planted failure is a false-success
control, and it costs one extra run.

39
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:05,440
A pass with a control
that failed as it should is evidence.

40
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:08,380
A pass on its own is only the absence
of a complaint.

41
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,067
A check earns trust by failing

42
00:02:11,067 --> 00:02:14,160
on something it should fail on,
and doing it on the record.

43
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:17,700
The voice you heard
is a synthesized version of mine.

44
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:21,200
Every number on screen links
to its source under the video.
